Explanation

## Purpose and entry points
This is a C-based neural-network inference/training library paired with weather-sensor datasets and serialized model artifacts. The operational entry point is  `operate.c`; deployed/generated weather model entry code is represented by `weatherPiModel.c` and `weatherPiModelContext.c`.

## Neural-network core
Model operates on arrays of pointers to allocated layer struct pointers(array of double pointers - so a triple pointer). Double pointer operations allow user to retain ownership of allocated memory as the library can provide functionality to operate and destroy layers according to the originals set up by the user and set those originals to NULL upon deletion - a design choice made specifically to prevent double frees and dangling pointers.

API was designed specifically to be conducive to flexible interconnections between layers so layers could connect to other layers and any individual layer could have multiple layers as previous layers and multiple layers as successive layers, thus allowing for implementation of complex model structures that can better capture specific niche features in datasets perhaps.

Model structure is implemented as a tree graph of layers, with the output layer as the root of the tree graph for the entire model to converge to. This allowed forward operations where calculations from successor layers required first the completion of calculations of predecessor layers to originally be done recursively in a post-order depth first search approach and backward operations which required the opposite to be done in a pre-order depth first search. 

However this was compute intensive and presented a challange to the implementation of very deep networks, so instead the model was reworked to adhere to data oriented design principles and contains a list of all the layers wherethe layers are sorted topologically, so all successive layers appear further along in the list than their predecessors, and the output layer serves as the ultimate layer. This introduced a sense of spatial locality of data when iterating through the layers and allowed for the advantage of operating on a single region of memory instead of playing with stack frames. A sort of bottom-up dynamic programming approach to the model operations - tabulation and iteration.

RNN functionality was also introduced by allowing time window/input layer pairs which hold data points and inputs from previous time steps but share weights with the layer they are extending context for

Model currently only supports glorot weight initialization. Further changes to be made are attention for RNNs, convolution, multidimensional inputs(for images - 3d, videos - 4d, #D Video - 5D), and LLM style self-attention. 

The model abstraction owns layers, construction/destruction, and model-level operations:
- `src/include/model.h`
- `src/include/model_construct.h`
- `src/include/model_ops.h`
- `src/include/model_destruct.h`

Layer lifecycle is separated from model lifecycle, enabling models to compose and release layer instances:
- `src/include/layer.h`
- `src/include/layer_construct.h`
- `src/include/layer_destruct.h`

## Compute and learning stages
Activation functions, loss functions, and numerical primitives form the math boundary used by layer/model operations:
- `src/include/activation.h`
- `src/include/loss.h`
- `src/include/nn_math.h`

Tensor/neural-network operations are centralized separately from the public model API:
- `src/include/nn_ops.h`
- `src/include/cml.h`
- `src/include/helper_funcs.h`

The expected control flow is model construction or CML loading → layer-forward operations → activation/loss/math routines → prediction or model update/output.

## Model serialization and deployment artifacts
`.cml` files are persisted model or context artifacts consumed by the CML/model layer. Several variants indicate iterative training on variations of models:
- `weathrModel.cml`
- `weathrModelContext.cml`
- `weathrPiModelBest.cml`
- `weathrPiModelContextBest.cml`

Generated C representations support environments where model training is done on a more powerful machine before being serialized and saved
- `weatherPiModel.c`
- `weatherPiModelContext.c`

## Sensor and embedded boundary
The sensor interface is isolated from NN internals, allowing sensor acquisition to feed normalized/model-ready input vectors:
- `src/include/sensor/sensor.h`
- `operate.c`
- `BME280 Sensor Manual.pdf`

The BME280 documentation suggests environmental inputs such as temperature, humidity, and pressure.

## Data preparation pipeline
Weather datasets encode raw and transformed feature sets. Python scripts impute the data and generate derivative datasets, for temporal deltas and normalization before model use:
- `dataset/thp.py`
- `dataset/thpdiffs.py`
- `dataset/thpdiffsnorm.py`

Key data boundaries are raw environmental samples (`dataset/THP.csv`), difference features (`dataset/THPDiffs.csv`), normalized differences (`dataset/THPDiffsNorm.csv`), and encoded training inputs (`dataset/THPOneHot.csv`).

## Validation assets
Standalone C test programs and accompanying full/sample CSV and CML fixtures exercise the NN library and weather-model loading paths:
- `src/tests/testWeatherModel.cml`
- `src/tests/THPDiffsFull.csv`
- `src/tests/THPOneHotFull.csv`

